# Employment Terms — Annex C: annual leave accrual

> Module LeaveAccrual

The module is declared `Leave_accrual` rather than `LeaveAccrual` because
Catala 1.2.1 requires the module name to match the file name up to casing
only, and the file name fixed by this repository's layout is
`leave_accrual.catala_en`. It is the `LeaveAccrual` module of
`catala/module_plan.md`.

Annex C legislates leave accrual one calendar month at a time: C-9.1 fixes the
rate for a completed calendar month and C-9.2 pro-rates the month in which
service begins or ends. `MonthlyAccrual` is therefore the unit, and the leave
year is the sum of its months. Carry-forward and forfeiture are a separate
question asked once per leave year, and `CarryForward` answers that one.

The rounding proviso of C-9.2 is given its own scope, `HalfDayRounding`. It is
lifted out because the proviso is a rule about a number and not about leave:
stating it separately is what makes the exact-quarter case reachable by a
test, and the exact-quarter case is the one the clause was written to settle.

## Prologue — declarations

```catala-metadata
declaration scope HalfDayRounding:
  input raw_days content decimal
  output half_steps content decimal
  output half_steps_below content decimal
  output excess_half_steps content decimal
  output is_exact_quarter content boolean
  output rounded_days content decimal

declaration scope MonthlyAccrual:
  input day_in_month content date
  input service_start content date
  input service_end content optional of date
  internal month_first_day content date
  internal month_last_day content date
  rounding scope HalfDayRounding
  output days_in_month content integer
  output days_of_service_in_month content integer
  output month_is_completed content boolean
  output pro_rated_days content decimal
  output days_accrued content decimal

declaration scope CarryForward:
  input accrued_untaken_days content decimal
  input prevented_from_taking_leave_by_long_term_sickness content boolean
  output permitted_carry_forward_days content decimal
  output carry_forward_days content decimal
  output forfeited_days content decimal
  output forfeited_days_payable_on_termination content boolean
```

## Calendar bounds of the month under examination

| NO-CLAUSE: C-9 speaks throughout of "a calendar month" and "that month".
| Deriving that month's first and last day from any day within it (the
| `day_in_month` input) is calendar
| decomposition of an input, not a rule of the Annex; both C-9.1 and C-9.2
| read the result. `date round down` is declared because Catala raises
| `AmbiguousDateComputation` in a scope with no rounding mode declared as soon
| as a month or year duration is added to a date — nothing below does so, and
| declaring the mode is what keeps that true under later edits.

```catala
scope MonthlyAccrual:
  date round down

scope MonthlyAccrual:
  definition month_first_day equals Date.first_day_of_month of day_in_month
  definition month_last_day equals Date.last_day_of_month of day_in_month
```

## C-9.1 Accrual for a completed calendar month

| EMP-ANNEX-C C-9.1 (001-employment-terms-annex-c.md:104)
|
| An Employee accrues annual leave at the rate of 2.0 days for each
| completed calendar month of service.

"Completed calendar month of service" is read as a calendar month throughout
which the Employee was in service — service beginning on or before the first
day of the month and not ending before its last day. The competing reading,
that a completed month runs from the anniversary of the start date, is
excluded by C-9.2: C-9.2 pro-rates by the days in the calendar month, which
only makes sense if the unit C-9.1 measures is the calendar month itself.

An open-ended engagement has no end date, which is why `service_end` is
optional; an Employee still in service has not terminated and so completes
every month from the month after commencement onwards.

Where there is an end date it cannot precede the start date, and C-9 is what
fixes that. Accrual under C-9.1 is "for each completed calendar month **of
service**", and C-9.2 pro-rates by "the number of days **of service** in that
month" where the Employee "commences or terminates employment" part-way through
it: the quantity both clauses measure is a period of service running from
commencement to termination. Employment cannot terminate before it commences,
so a `service_end` earlier than `service_start` bounds no period of service and
neither clause has anything to measure. The scope refuses it instead of
answering nought days, which is what the arithmetic would otherwise return —
an answer indistinguishable from the lawful case of a month falling wholly
outside a real period of service. The bound is the clauses', not a programming
convenience.

The boundary is made explicit as `month_is_completed`, and it is this variable
alone that separates C-9.1 from C-9.2. A month in which service begins on the
second day, or ends on the penultimate day, is not a completed month and
accrues under C-9.2 even where the pro-rated figure happens to round back to
2.0 days.

```catala
scope MonthlyAccrual:
  assertion
    match service_end with pattern
    -- Absent: true
    -- Present content ending: ending >= service_start

  definition month_is_completed equals
    service_start <= month_first_day
    and (match service_end with pattern
         -- Absent: true
         -- Present content ending: ending >= month_last_day)

  label c9_1 definition days_accrued
    under condition month_is_completed
    consequence equals 2.0
```

## C-9.2 Pro-rating a part month

| EMP-ANNEX-C C-9.2 (001-employment-terms-annex-c.md:107)
|
| Where an Employee commences or terminates employment part-way through a
| calendar month, accrual in respect of that month is pro-rated by reference
| to the number of days of service in that month as a proportion of the
| number of days in that month, rounded to the nearest half day, with an
| exact quarter rounded upwards.

C-9.2 is an exception to C-9.1 and is encoded as one. Its condition is the
exact complement of C-9.1's, so there is nothing of the parent's condition to
repeat: a Catala exception does not inherit its parent's condition, and here
the child's condition is the parent's negation rather than a narrowing of it.

The denominator is "the number of days in that month" — 28, 29, 30 or 31, read
off the month under examination and never a constant. Two months of identical
service length therefore accrue different amounts: eighteen days of service in
February 2023 accrues 1.5 days and the same eighteen days in February 2024
accrues 1.0, purely because the denominator is 28 in one year and 29 in the
other. Both are tested.

The numerator counts whole days of service falling in the month, inclusive of
the first and last such day, which is why the count is a difference plus one.
Where service does not overlap the month at all the numerator is zero and the
month accrues nothing; that case is outside the words "commences or terminates
employment part-way through a calendar month" but is the arithmetic the clause
supplies for it, and it is tested.

```catala
scope MonthlyAccrual:
  definition days_in_month equals Date.get_day of month_last_day

  definition days_of_service_in_month equals
    let period_start equals Date.max of service_start, month_first_day in
    let period_end equals
      (match service_end with pattern
       -- Absent: month_last_day
       -- Present content ending: Date.min of ending, month_last_day) in
    if period_end < period_start then 0
    else integer of ((period_end - period_start) / (1 day)) + 1

  definition rounding.raw_days equals
    2.0 * (decimal of days_of_service_in_month)
        / (decimal of days_in_month)

  definition pro_rated_days equals rounding.rounded_days

  label c9_2 exception c9_1 definition days_accrued
    under condition not month_is_completed
    consequence equals pro_rated_days
```

### The rounding proviso of C-9.2

An exact quarter is any odd multiple of a quarter day — 0.25, 0.75, 1.25 and
1.75 within the 0-to-2-day range a month can produce — because each is exactly
midway between two half days and so is exactly the case "nearest" cannot
decide. The proviso rounds every one of them upwards: 0.25 becomes 0.5 and, by
the same words, 0.75 becomes 1.0, 1.25 becomes 1.5 and 1.75 becomes 2.0. The
narrower reading, that "an exact quarter" means only the value 0.25 and that
0.75 is therefore something else, would leave 0.75 with no rule at all, which
cannot be what the proviso is for.

A finding worth stating: on real calendar months the proviso is inoperative.
The unrounded accrual is 2d/n for d days of service in a month of n days, and
2d/n is an odd multiple of a quarter only if 8 divides n. No calendar month
has 28, 29, 30 or 31 days, so none is divisible by 8, and the exact-quarter
case cannot arise from `MonthlyAccrual` at all. It is encoded faithfully
anyway, because a change to the C-9.1 rate or to the pro-rating base would
make it bite, and it is tested directly against `HalfDayRounding`, where it is
reachable.

| EMP-ANNEX-C C-9.2 (001-employment-terms-annex-c.md:107)
|
| Where an Employee commences or terminates employment part-way through a
| calendar month, accrual in respect of that month is pro-rated by reference
| to the number of days of service in that month as a proportion of the
| number of days in that month, rounded to the nearest half day, with an
| exact quarter rounded upwards.

"Rounded to the nearest half day" is the rule; "with an exact quarter rounded
upwards" is a proviso resolving the only case in which "nearest" has no
answer, and it is encoded as an exception to the rule rather than folded into
it.

Working in half-days makes both limbs visible. `half_steps` is the unrounded
accrual expressed in half-days, `half_steps_below` is the half-day at or below
it, and `excess_half_steps` is the remaining fraction of a half-day, which
lies in [0, 1). The nearest half day is the one below when that fraction is
under a half and the one above when it is over. The fraction is exactly a half
precisely when the unrounded accrual is an exact quarter of a day.

```catala
scope HalfDayRounding:
  definition half_steps equals raw_days * 2.0

  definition half_steps_below equals
    Decimal.round_by_default of half_steps

  definition excess_half_steps equals half_steps - half_steps_below

  definition is_exact_quarter equals excess_half_steps = 0.5

  label nearest_half definition rounded_days
    under condition excess_half_steps < 0.5
    consequence equals half_steps_below / 2.0

  label nearest_half definition rounded_days
    under condition excess_half_steps > 0.5
    consequence equals (half_steps_below + 1.0) / 2.0

```

## C-9.3 Carry-forward cap

| EMP-ANNEX-C C-9.3 (001-employment-terms-annex-c.md:113)
|
| An Employee may carry forward to the next leave year a maximum of 5
| accrued but untaken days.

C-9.3 does two things: it fixes the amount that may be carried forward at 5
days, and it caps the carry-forward at the accrued but untaken balance where
that balance is smaller. The permitted amount is held in its own variable
because C-9.4 replaces it and C-9.5 must read whatever it ends up being.

```catala
scope CarryForward:
  label c9_3 definition permitted_carry_forward_days equals 5.0

  definition carry_forward_days equals
    Decimal.min of accrued_untaken_days, permitted_carry_forward_days
```

## C-9.4 Long-term sickness absence

| EMP-ANNEX-C C-9.4 (001-employment-terms-annex-c.md:116)
|
| By way of exception to C-9.3, an Employee who was prevented from taking
| accrued leave by reason of long-term sickness absence may carry forward
| the whole of their accrued but untaken balance.

C-9.4 is `HYBRID` in the adjudicated ledger and its trigger is a judgement
input. Whether an Employee "was prevented from taking accrued leave" and
whether that was "by reason of long-term sickness absence" are both causal
determinations the Annex does not define; neither reduces to a record the way
a job grade or an incident severity does. The judgement is therefore lifted to
the scope input `prevented_from_taking_leave_by_long_term_sickness`, and the
module computes the consequence given that judgement without ever making it.

The consequence is arithmetic: the permitted carry-forward becomes the whole
accrued but untaken balance in place of the 5 days of C-9.3. Because C-9.3's
figure is unconditional there is no parent condition for this exception to
repeat.

```catala
scope CarryForward:
  label c9_4 exception c9_3 definition permitted_carry_forward_days
    under condition prevented_from_taking_leave_by_long_term_sickness
    consequence equals accrued_untaken_days
```

## C-9.5 Forfeiture

| EMP-ANNEX-C C-9.5 (001-employment-terms-annex-c.md:120)
|
| Accrued but untaken leave in excess of the amount permitted to be carried
| forward is forfeited at the end of the leave year and is not payable on
| termination.

C-9.5 reads the permitted amount that C-9.3 and C-9.4 between them produce; it
does not restate the 5 days. Where C-9.4 applies, the permitted amount is the
whole balance, nothing is in excess of it, and nothing is forfeited — the
exception would leak if the forfeiture were computed against a hard 5.

The second limb is a rule about the forfeited days rather than about their
number: they are not payable on termination. It is encoded as such, so that a
termination calculation reading this scope is told that the forfeited figure
carries no money rather than having to infer it.

```catala
scope CarryForward:
  definition forfeited_days equals
    accrued_untaken_days - carry_forward_days

  definition forfeited_days_payable_on_termination equals false
```
